  "notes": "A godan verb (む row) derived from {汗|あせ} (sweat). Describes the onset of light perspiration rather than heavy sweating. Often used in descriptive or literary writing to convey physical sensation.\n\nUSAGE:\nThe ている form ({汗|あせ}ばんでいる) describes an ongoing state of being slightly sweaty. The attributive form ({汗|あせ}ばむ{肌|はだ}, sweaty skin) is common in descriptive prose.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {額|ひたい}が{汗|あせ}ばむ — one's forehead gets sweaty\n- {手|て}が{汗|あせ}ばむ — one's hands get sweaty\n- {汗|あせ}ばむ{陽気|ようき} — sweaty weather\n- うっすら{汗|あせ}ばむ — to perspire faintly\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {汗|あせ}をかく — to sweat; the most common everyday expression for sweating, while {汗|あせ}ばむ emphasizes the gradual onset and lighter degree",
